---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const P1 = `let names = ["Avery", "Blake", "Casey"];

console.log(names[0]);
console.log(names.length);`;
const P2 = `aList <- [10, 20, 30]
APPEND(aList, 40)
INSERT(aList, 2, 15)
REMOVE(aList, 1)
DISPLAY(LENGTH(aList))
DISPLAY(aList[1])`;
const P3 = `first <- [1, 2, 3]
second <- first
second[1] <- 99

DISPLAY(first[1])
DISPLAY(second[1])`;
const RefCard = `a <- expression          assign a copy of the result to a
DISPLAY(expression)      show the value, FOLLOWED BY A SPACE
INPUT()                  take a value from the user

+  -  *  /               17 / 5 is 3.4  (no truncation)
a MOD b                  remainder.  17 MOD 5 is 2
RANDOM(a, b)             whole number from a to b, BOTH INCLUDED

=  ≠  >  <  ≥  ≤            comparison.  = means EQUALS
NOT / AND / OR           boolean operators

IF(condition)            no ELSE IF exists - nest an IF inside an ELSE
{ ... }
ELSE
{ ... }

REPEAT n TIMES           no counter variable is provided
{ ... }
REPEAT UNTIL(condition)  repeats UNTIL true - the inverse of while
{ ... }

aList <- [v1, v2, v3]    FIRST ELEMENT IS aList[1]
aList <- bList           assigns a COPY of bList
LENGTH(aList)            number of elements
APPEND(aList, value)     add to the end, length + 1
INSERT(aList, i, value)  shift right from i, place value at i
REMOVE(aList, i)         delete index i, shift left, length - 1
FOR EACH item IN aList   item takes each value, first to last
{ ... }

An index below 1 or above LENGTH ends the program with an error.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Pseudocode Challenge: Lists</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>Three problems on lists. This is where the two notations disagree most, and both disagreements are on this page: the exam sheet counts from <b>1</b>, and <code>&larr;</code> makes a <b>copy</b> where JavaScript would share.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Do this on paper.</b> There is nothing to run and nothing to type.
            On exam day you get this notation on a printed sheet and no
            computer, so practicing it any other way practices the wrong thing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="reference">
          <h2>Reference Card</h2>

          <p>
            Everything you need for this page. This is the same notation the
            College Board uses on the exam.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={RefCard}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Exam Notation — what you have so far"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Two things catch people every time.</b>{" "}
            <code>DISPLAY</code> puts <b>a space</b> after whatever it shows, so
            two <code>DISPLAY</code> calls in a row give you
            <code>A B</code> on one line &mdash; not two lines and not
            <code>AB</code>. And <code>=</code> means <b>equals</b> here, not
            assignment. Assignment is <code>&larr;</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="problems">
          <h2>The Problems</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 1 &mdash; translate</h3>

          <p>Rewrite this in exam notation. Two things need to change and neither is the loop.</p>

          <Code
            code={P1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Problem 1"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 2 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>Give the contents of <code>aList</code> after each line runs, and then the final output.</p>

          <Code
            code={P2}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 3 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>What does this display?</p>

          <Code
            code={P3}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 3"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p><b>Then answer this:</b> the same program written in JavaScript, with <code>let second = first;</code> and <code>second[0] = 99;</code>, displays something different. Say what, and why.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Your three answers. Problem 2 asks for the list contents at each step; show all of them.</p>

          <p>
            Worth <b>3 points</b>, graded on completion. Show your working for
            the traces &mdash; a wrong answer with visible reasoning is worth
            more to both of us than a right one with none.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
